      {
        "slug": "intro-to-financial-markets",
        "title": "Introduction to Financial Markets",
        "date": "2026-07-27",
        "summary": "A first look at how markets price risk, time, and information \u2014 the vocabulary the rest of this research assumes.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "Every financial market exists to do one thing: let people trade claims on future, uncertain outcomes today, at an agreed price. A stock is a claim on a company's future profits. A bond is a claim on a stream of future payments. A derivative, covered in a later chapter, is a claim on some other claim."
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "content": "Price, and why it moves"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "A market price is just the point where a buyer and a seller currently agree. It moves because new information changes what people think a future outcome is worth, or because someone needs liquidity right now and is willing to pay for it. The plot below is a toy simulation of that idea \u2014 not a forecast, just the shape of a price path under random daily moves."
          },
